.TH CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_destroy \- destroy a chttpsvr handle and set it to CHTTPSVR_INVALID
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_destroy(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the server that name holds and then sets name to
CHTTPSVR_INVALID.

It drains every in-flight request and closes every remaining idle
connection.  Connections that wait without a thread are ended as well:
a parked body or response is closed, a request that waits for body
memory is closed, and a streaming request that waits in the streaming
queue gets 503 Service Unavailable.  When this is the last active
server, it also stops the shared engine.  The listener socket is
closed as part of this, so calling chttpsvr_stop first is optional.

A second call on a separate copy of the same handle value is a FATAL
ERROR, whether it is concurrent or comes later, after the first call
has completed, and so is a call from inside a request handler or a
middleware of this server.  The library reports the error at once with
abort() and SIGABRT, instead of a silent double-free or a hang.  To
shut a server down from inside one of its own handlers, destroy it
from a different thread, or call chttpsvr_engine_stop().

It is safe to call this macro whether or not the shared engine is
still running.  It frees the engine reference of name, which can start
an asynchronous engine stop when it was the last reference.  It is
also safe when chttpsvr_engine_stop() has already force-stopped the
engine while name was still started: the engine stop has then already
quiesced this server, so this call finds the teardown of the server
done and frees the remaining resources of the server.  When such a
force-stop is still tearing this same server down on its own
background thread, this call waits for that teardown to complete
instead of racing it.

This macro only STARTS that asynchronous engine stop.  The destroy of
the reactor, the stop of the idle sweep and the close of the fallback
engine logger all run on a separate reaper thread after this call
returns, and every guarantee of this call covers name alone: its
listener is closed, its connections are closed, and its worker pool
is drained.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() after it whenever that final
teardown must finish before the next step.  Always call it before you
return from main() and before you unload this library from an image that
dlopen() loaded.  Also call it before you tear down a custom allocator
installed with chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(), because
the reaper frees the memory of the reactor and of the fallback engine
logger through that allocator.

The reaper thread itself needs no such call: an application that
destroys its last server and then calls neither chttpsvr_engine_wait()
nor a later chttpsvr_start() leaves no unjoined thread behind, and no
thread stack mapping.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates name exactly once.  name must be a modifiable
lvalue, such as a variable or an array element, and an expression
with a side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears exactly
one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
